  <data name="Alphabet_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Alphabet &amp; Sounds</value></data>
  <data name="Alphabet_Intro" xml:space="preserve"><value>Megruli (მარგალური/მეგრული) is written with the Georgian script and shares almost all of its letters and sounds with Georgian. A Georgian speaker can read Megruli text immediately — the differences are mostly in vocabulary and grammar, plus two extra sounds described below.</value></data>
  <data name="Alphabet_VowelsTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Vowels</value></data>
  <data name="Alphabet_VowelsP1" xml:space="preserve"><value>Megruli has five main vowels, same as Georgian: ა, ე, ი, ო, უ.</value></data>
  <data name="Alphabet_VowelsP2" xml:space="preserve"><value>It also has one extra, secondary vowel used mainly in the Zugdidi–Samurzakano dialect: ჷ — the result of a weakened ი or უ.</value></data>
  <data name="Alphabet_VowelsP3" xml:space="preserve"><value>ჷ corresponds to the IPA schwa sound [ə] — roughly the sound between the "d" and "m" in the English word freedom. In the Senaki–Martvili dialect, ო, ი or უ are used instead.</value></data>
  <data name="Alphabet_ConsonantsTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Consonants</value></data>
  <data name="Alphabet_ConsonantsIntro" xml:space="preserve"><value>The consonant inventory is nearly identical to Georgian and Laz. Two sounds are worth calling out:</value></data>
  <data name="Alphabet_ConsonantY" xml:space="preserve"><value>ჸ — a sound that doesn't exist in standard Georgian or Laz. Examples: თამაში → ლაჸაფი (game/play), სახლი → ჸუდე (house).</value></data>
  <data name="Alphabet_ConsonantQ" xml:space="preserve"><value>ყ — appears mostly next to წ, ჭ, ტ. Examples: ჭყონი (oak), ტყა (forest).</value></data>
  <data name="Alphabet_TypingTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Typing ჷ and ჸ</value></data>
  <data name="Alphabet_TypingBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>These two letters aren't on a standard Georgian keyboard layout. On Windows, with a Georgian keyboard layout active, they're commonly available via Right Alt + J (ჷ) and Left Alt + Y (ჸ), or by installing a font such as DejaVu Sans that includes full Georgian Unicode coverage.</value></data>
